1.1.text:
Article 3

The objectives of protection of victims of gender-based violence and children are:

a. to prevent all forms of gender-based violence and violence against children, occurring within the scope of the household and/or community; b. to provide protection;
c. to provide legal assistance;
d. to pursue recovery and social reintegration; and
e. to increase community participation.

Article 5

(1) The obligations of the Regional Government include:

a. preventing the occurrence of violence;
b. providing protection for victims of violence;
c. providing recovery services and social reintegration;
d. encouraging and increasing community participation;
e. conducting cooperation with service providers in efforts to prevent, protect and recover victims of violence.
(2) To carry out the obligations as referred to in paragraph (1), the Regional Government has the authority to:
a. formulate policies and programs on the elimination of gender-based violence and children;
b. establish integrated services and KPK2BGA;
c. facilitate the implementation of integrated services and KPK2BGA activities;
d. provide facilities and infrastructure;
e. enhance the capacity of service provider institutions;
f. conduct coordination and cooperation in the implementation of protection for victims of gender-based violence and children; g. encourage community participation;
h. conduct monitoring and evaluation.
(3) The regional government in carrying out the obligations as referred to in paragraph (1) and paragraph (2) must take into account the rights and obligations of parents, guardians, husbands, wives or other persons who are legally responsible to the victim.

Article 23

(1) The community may participate in assisting prevention and handling efforts for victims of gender-based and child violence.

(2) Community participation as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be realized through:

a. providing information and or reporting any violence that is known;
b. providing protection for victims;
c. providing emergency assistance;
d. providing advocacy to victims and or the community regarding the handling of gender-based and child violence cases;
e. assisting in the process of submitting applications for protection orders; and f. assisting in the repatriation and social reintegration process.

Article 4

(1) Every victim of gender-based violence and child victim of violence has the right to:

a. obtain protection for the security of their person, family, and property, and freedom from threats related to testimony that will be, is being, or has been given;
b. participate in the process of selecting and determining the form of protection and security support;
c. freedom from ensnaring questions;
d. receive information regarding case developments and court decisions;
e. receive services that are prompt, appropriate, comfortable, and according to needs;
f. recovery and social reintegration;
g. receive legal, psychological assistance, spiritual guidance, economic, social and interpreter assistance.
(2) The rights of victims as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be implemented in accordance with the provisions of applicable laws and regulations.

